/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An SCR is latched while its anode current stays above the holding current IH: the two internal transistors keep each other on.
   - The device turn-off time tq (from the datasheet) is the time from current zero until the SCR can block forward voltage again; it is about 10 µs to 20 µs for fast (inverter-grade) SCRs and 50 µs to a few hundred microseconds for converter-gr
   - for a capacitor of C farads, charged to V volts, placed across the SCR to commutate a load current of I amperes that stays constant during commutation (true for an inductive load).
   - In class C with resistive loads R1 and R2, the capacitor voltage seen by the SCR being turned off swings from −V towards +V through R1, so tc = R1 C ln 2 = 0.693 R1 C, not C V / I.
*/
