/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A TRIAC (triode AC switch) behaves like two SCRs in inverse parallel on one chip with a common gate.
   - It is triggered in four modes, named by the sign of MT2 and of the gate, both measured from MT1: I+ (MT2 +, G +), I− (MT2 +, G −), III− (MT2 −, G −) and III+ (MT2 −, G +).
   - With an inductive load the current lags the voltage, so when the TRIAC current reaches zero the supply voltage is already large and reappears across it very fast.
   - A DIAC is a two-terminal, bidirectional trigger diode with no gate.
   - In a lamp dimmer a variable resistor R and a capacitor C form a phase-shifting network across the supply.
   - A GTO has the same p-n-p-n layers as an SCR, but the cathode is split into many narrow fingers surrounded by gate metal (interdigitated), and the p-n-p part has a lower gain.
   - βoff = IA / IG(off)
   - where βoff is the turn-off gain (no unit), IA the anode current to be turned off and IG(off) the peak negative gate current, both in amperes. βoff is only about 3 to 5.
   - The low turn-off gain is the GTO's price: a 600 A GTO needs a negative gate pulse of 120 A to 200 A, from a low-impedance drive.
*/
