/* book Worked example: Q: A TRIAC dimmer on a 230 V, 50 Hz supply fires at α = 90° in both half-cycles into a 100 Ω resistive lamp. Find the rms load voltage and the lamp power, using Vrms = Vs √(1 − α/π + sin 2α/(2π)).
   1. α = 90° = π/2 rad, so α/π = 0.5 and sin 2α = sin 180° = 0.
   2. Vrms = 230 × √(1 − 0.5 + 0) = 230 × 0.7071 = 162.6 V.
   3. P = Vrms² / R = 230² × 0.5 ÷ 100 = 26 450 ÷ 100 = 264.5 W.
   4. Check: at α = 0 the lamp would take 230² ÷ 100 = 529 W, and firing at 90° removes the first half of each half-cycle, which carries exactly half the energy of a sine wave.
   Answer: Vrms = 162.6 V and P = 264.5 W, half of the full 529 W. */
