/* book exam traps: MT1 and MT2 are not anode and cathode; current flows either way. The gate and MT2 polarities are always measured from MT1. | The DIAC only triggers; the TRIAC carries the lamp current. The RC network, not the DIAC, sets the firing angle. | Writing that a TRIAC is equally sensitive in all four modes: III+ needs the most gate current. | GTO turn-off is not a small signal: with βoff = 4, a 600 A GTO needs 150 A of negative gate current. | A TRIAC on an inductive load can fail to turn off because of the commutating dv/dt; the fix is a snubber, not a bigger gate pulse. */
