/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A power BJT is a vertical n+ emitter, p base, n− drift, n+ collector structure.
   - Its weak point is second breakdown: current crowds into a hot spot, which takes still more current and fails, even below rated power, so the safe operating area (SOA) bounds the allowed VCE–IC path.
   - A power MOSFET is a vertical n+ source, p body, n− drift, n+ drain structure with an insulated gate.
   - The gate sees Cgs, Cgd (the Miller capacitance) and Cds; datasheets give Ciss = Cgs + Cgd, Coss = Cds + Cgd, Crss = Cgd and a gate charge curve.
   - Switching loss happens in the current-rise and voltage-fall intervals; a stronger gate current shortens them.
   - An IGBT is a MOSFET structure with an extra p+ layer at the collector.
   - For conduction alone, MOSFET loss I² RDS(on) is a parabola and IGBT loss about VCE(sat) I a straight line; they are equal at I = VCE(sat) / RDS(on), and below that the MOSFET wins.
*/
