/* book Worked example 2: Q: A MOSFET switches a 10 A clamped inductive load from 200 V at 100 kHz, with its gate driven from 0 to 12 V through 10 Ω in total. The datasheet gives VGS(th) = 4 V, plateau Vpl = 6 V, gate charge to the plateau Qgs = 14 nC, Qgd = 18 nC and total Qg = 50 nC. Estimate td(on), tri, tfv, the turn-on energy and loss, and the gate drive power.
   1. Before the plateau the gate acts as Ciss ≈ Qgs / Vpl = 14 nC ÷ 6 V = 2.33 nF, so τ = 10 Ω × 2.33 nF = 23.3 ns. vGS = 12(1 − e−t/τ) reaches 4 V at td(on) = 23.3 × ln 1.5 = 9.5 ns and 6 V at 23.3 × ln 2 = 16.2 ns, so tri = 6.7 ns.
   2. On the plateau: Ig = (12 − 6) ÷ 10 = 0.6 A, so tfv = Qgd / Ig = 18 nC ÷ 0.6 A = 30 ns.
   3. Turn-on energy: Eon ≈ ½ × 200 V × 10 A × (6.7 + 30) ns = 36.7 µJ; at 100 kHz, Pon = 36.7 µJ × 100 000 = 3.67 W.
   4. Gate drive power: Qg Vdrive fs = 50 nC × 12 V × 100 000 = 0.06 W.
   Answer: td(on) ≈ 9.5 ns, tri ≈ 6.7 ns, tfv = 30 ns; Eon ≈ 36.7 µJ (3.7 W at 100 kHz); gate power 60 mW. The Miller plateau takes most of the switching time. */
